WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:03.170
I would rather have a conversation.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.760
I would rather spend time, you know,
acquiring information, listening to

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.100
interesting podcasts and then talk about
what I've been listening to and reading.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:16.820
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
and today I want to talk about

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.070
task-based language learning.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.950
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.485
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:25.785 --> 00:00:29.445
Uh, if you follow me on a podcast
service, uh, please leave a review.

00:00:29.445 --> 00:00:30.435
I do appreciate it.

00:00:31.335 --> 00:00:33.225
So task-based language learning.

00:00:33.675 --> 00:00:38.295
The really, the reason I raise this
subject is because I realized just

00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:43.215
how important it is in language
learning to have specific tasks.

00:00:43.785 --> 00:00:49.199
Um, I noticed this, that if I
assign myself a task or if I feel

00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:54.179
an obligation to do something, or if
someone assigns a task to me, then

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:58.319
I'm likely to do it simply because
it's, it's easy to have a task.

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:02.790
If my wife says, you know,
chop these vegetables.

00:01:02.819 --> 00:01:04.170
Okay, chop the vegetables.

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:07.770
It's, it helps it's, you don't have to
kind of think what should I be doing now?

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.590
You have a task, and the
same with language learning.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.609
So first thing in the morning, uh,
typically I'll listen to a podcast

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:20.340
and then I'll try and grab the URL if
it's on YouTube and somehow get an MP3

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.410
file so that I can get it transcribed.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.789
So now I've got this task that's
developing, I'm listening to this podcast.

00:01:26.789 --> 00:01:28.380
I'm, I've got the transcript.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.670
I'm studying a transcript onLingQ
without even having to think about it.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.380
I have this task that I have to do.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.970
Um, if my tutor worked to
assign me a task, here are three

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.590
lessons I want you to read them.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.920
I want you to save words and phrases or,
uh, when we're on our 90-Day Challenge,

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.720
I'm trying to keep to a certain level in
terms of LingQs created are words known.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:57.060
These are very specific things over
and above my general interest in the

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:03.090
language, specific tasks that help
get me going and like so much in life,

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.010
life it's important to get going.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.450
It's important to do something.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.060
If you do something, it
leads to other things.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:14.250
So tasks I think are very important
to keep us, you know, going forward

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.870
in our language learning activities.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.250
But when you say task-based language
learning, if you Google for it,

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.440
you'll find a different definition.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.370
And that task-based language instruction
is a method of language instruction, where

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:35.700
as I understand it, um, the learner is
asked to perform a task in the language,

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:42.750
uh, not to worry about grammar structures
or vocabulary and to incorporate, you

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:48.480
know, hand gestures or whatever in order
to accomplish a task in the language.

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:53.339
Now, I, I don't know whether this is an
effective way of teaching the language.

00:02:53.579 --> 00:02:55.049
To me, it's a little artificial.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.839
Uh, it's a bit like this
role playing thing, which I

00:02:57.839 --> 00:02:59.429
also don't particularly like.

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:01.440
I would rather have a conversation.

00:03:01.769 --> 00:03:06.045
I would rather spend time, you know,
acquiring information, listening to

00:03:06.045 --> 00:03:10.575
interesting podcasts, and then talk
about what I've been listening to

00:03:10.575 --> 00:03:15.295
and reading and sort of gradually
see my vocabulary increase, my

00:03:15.345 --> 00:03:16.985
comprehension  level increase.

00:03:17.235 --> 00:03:21.105
I'm not that interested in, in engaging
in these artificial activities.

00:03:21.765 --> 00:03:27.495
The, uh, one example of the application
of this task-based language instruction,

00:03:28.785 --> 00:03:31.155
and of course in the field of
language instruction there are all

00:03:31.155 --> 00:03:37.320
kinds of, you know, the latest, you
know, fad, uh, as I see it, uh...

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.330
I'm with Krashen, I think language
instruction comes down to comprehensible,

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.590
compelling input, massive listening and
reading, allowing the brain to get used

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:52.830
to the language and everything else is
kind of refinements, which are either

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:57.270
interesting or not very interesting,
but maybe not all that necessary.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.800
One application is in Canada we
have a thing called the 12 language

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:06.810
benchmarks, which again yet another way
of grading people's language skills.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:10.800
So we have TOEFL and TOEIC
and IELTS and Cambridge.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.860
So there's another one,
Canadian Language Benchmarks.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:20.399
There are 12 levels and each level
tries to describe in terms of, you

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:24.990
know, listening comprehension, reading
comprehension, speaking, and writing,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.090
you know, what the person's level is.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.970
Well, level one, theycan't do much.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.090
So, you know, can hardly say anything.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:34.110
Okay, that's level one.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:38.550
Level two, can hardly say anything, but
can say a little more than level one.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.600
In other words, all of the detailed
description of what this person is able

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.900
to do is relatively meaningless to me.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:51.620
The person is gradually getting better,
hopefully, and there are no clear,

00:04:52.335 --> 00:04:57.284
you know, demarcation lines between
level three and level four and five.

00:04:57.465 --> 00:05:01.005
That's why beginner, intermediate
and advanced is pretty

00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:02.205
good because it's simple.

00:05:02.294 --> 00:05:03.435
It's a fluid thing.

00:05:03.435 --> 00:05:07.755
It's not like there are steps like
this that we go through and we may be

00:05:07.784 --> 00:05:12.315
progressing more quickly in comprehension,
not as quickly in speaking or writing,

00:05:12.315 --> 00:05:13.965
or we may not even bother with writing.

00:05:14.234 --> 00:05:19.875
And so trying to say that at each, you
know, benchmark level here is a clear

00:05:19.875 --> 00:05:21.525
definition of what this person can do.

00:05:21.975 --> 00:05:26.865
Now, the reason for this is that for
this Canadian Language Benchmark system,

00:05:26.895 --> 00:05:32.895
they, they hope that, um, immigrants,
for example, can demonstrate the ability

00:05:32.895 --> 00:05:37.935
to perform, uh, tasks related to being
a clerk, uh, you know, in a supermarket

00:05:37.935 --> 00:05:42.915
or a receptionist at a hotel, uh,
jobs that are considered to be less

00:05:42.945 --> 00:05:44.805
demanding when it comes to language.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:49.680
You know, jobs that immigrants could
easily fill once they achieved that level.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:56.520
Now of course, here again, uh, I
have, uh, met many checkout clerks

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:00.750
at supermarkets, many of whom are
immigrants, they all speak quite well.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.750
I mean, the, the ones that are good
at it, they can actually entertain

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.490
conversations on different subjects.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:12.360
I don't think it's that useful to try
to just train yourself on the limited

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.159
number of words you need for that.

00:06:15.044 --> 00:06:17.505
A receptionist at a hotel even more.

00:06:17.505 --> 00:06:22.815
So I think it's far better to focus
on improving your overall level in the

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:26.864
language, acquiring more words, doing
more listening and reading and not

00:06:26.864 --> 00:06:33.075
pretending that just because you act
out certain roles or certain tasks that

00:06:33.075 --> 00:06:34.934
that's going to prepare you for that task.

00:06:35.085 --> 00:06:39.225
You're better to continue to
improve your comprehension through

00:06:39.225 --> 00:06:40.635
lots of listening and reading.

00:06:40.905 --> 00:06:44.625
And if you have that base,
even if when you start in a

00:06:44.625 --> 00:06:46.665
particular job, you struggle.

00:06:46.755 --> 00:06:50.145
If you have that comprehension,
you have the vocabulary you

00:06:50.145 --> 00:06:51.555
will eventually improve.

00:06:51.765 --> 00:06:52.905
And it really doesn't matter.

00:06:53.685 --> 00:06:58.065
You know where you are, theoretically
in the sort of steps of the 12

00:06:58.065 --> 00:07:02.685
language benchmarks, what matters is,
how do we get the, get the person?

00:07:02.775 --> 00:07:04.365
How do we encourage that person?

00:07:04.604 --> 00:07:07.784
How do we make it easier for that
person to continue to improve

00:07:07.905 --> 00:07:09.224
and get better and better?

00:07:09.284 --> 00:07:16.375
And I'm not sure that the sort of
Canadian Language Benchmark task-based

00:07:16.395 --> 00:07:17.685
instruction is the way to go.

00:07:17.805 --> 00:07:22.065
On the other hand, assigning yourself
tasks like doing a lot of listening

00:07:22.185 --> 00:07:26.595
and reading and speaking is probably
going to get you there sooner.

00:07:26.745 --> 00:07:28.845
So maybe a little bit controversial.

00:07:28.845 --> 00:07:32.415
I'm sure I'll get some pushback
from professional language teachers.

00:07:32.625 --> 00:07:33.015
Okay.

00:07:33.045 --> 00:07:33.885
Thanks for listening.

00:07:33.915 --> 00:07:34.005
Bye.

